msgid "HTTP reference"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This document clarifies different HTTP functionalities available in Quarkus."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Eclipse Vert.x supplies the fundamental HTTP layer.\n"
"For Servlet support, Quarkus employs a customized Undertow version that "
"operates on top of Vert.x, while Jakarta REST support is delivered through "
"RESTEasy."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"When Undertow is present, RESTEasy functions as a Servlet filter.\n"
"In its absence, RESTEasy operates directly on Vert.x without involving "
"Servlets."
msgstr ""

msgid "Serving static resources"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If you are looking to use Quarkus for a web application, look at the xref:"
"web.adoc[Quarkus for the Web] guide."
msgstr ""

msgid "From the application jar"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To serve static resources from the application jar, you must place them in "
"the `META-INF/resources` directory of your application. This location\n"
"was chosen as it is the standard location for resources in `jar` files as "
"defined by the Servlet spec. Even though\n"
"Quarkus can be used without Servlet, following this convention allows "
"existing code that places its resources in this\n"
"location to function correctly."
msgstr ""

msgid "From web dependencies like webjars or mvnpm"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Look at the xref:web-dependency-locator.adoc[Web dependency locator] guide "
"for details on how to use https://www.webjars.org[WebJars], https://mvnpm."
"org[mvnpm] and https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Element/"
"script/type/importmap[importmaps]"
msgstr ""

msgid "From a local directory"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Static resources can be served from a local directory by installing an "
"additional route in the Vert.x router."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"For instance, to serve resources from the `static/` directory relative to "
"the current path at http://localhost:8080/static/, you can install the "
"following route:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Local filesystem static resources"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Quarkus can serve static files from a local filesystem directory, outside of "
"the application JAR and Java resources.\n"
"This is configured via the `quarkus.http.static-dir.*` properties."
msgstr ""

msgid "To expose the contents of a `static/` directory:"
msgstr ""

msgid "By default, static files are exposed under the `/static` endpoint."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"With this configuration, a file located at `static/picture.jpg` will be "
"available at:\n"
"`http://localhost:8080/static/picture.jpg`."
msgstr ""

msgid "To change the default endpoint:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"With this configuration, a file located at `static/picture.jpg` will be "
"available at:\n"
"`http://localhost:8080/picture.jpg`."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The `quarkus.http.static-dir.endpoint` value must not contain `*` or `..`, "
"and it will be normalized\n"
"to avoid trailing slashes.\n"
"Similarly, `quarkus.http.static-dir.path` must be a relative path without `.."
"`.\n"
"Quarkus validates these values at build time."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This feature can be used in both development and production environments."
msgstr ""

msgid "HTTP Compression"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The response body of a static resource is not compressed by default.\n"
"You can enable the HTTP compression support by means of `quarkus.http.enable-"
"compression=true`.\n"
"If compression support is enabled then the response body is compressed if "
"the `Content-Type` header derived from the file name of a resource is a "
"compressed media type as configured via `quarkus.http.compress-media-types`."
msgstr ""

msgid "Other Configurations"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Additionally, the index page for static resources can be changed from "
"default `index.html`, the hidden files (e.g. dot files) can be indicated as "
"not served, the range requests can be disabled, and the caching support (e.g."
" caching headers and file properties cache) can be configured."
msgstr ""

msgid "Configuring the Context path"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"By default, Quarkus will serve content from under the root context. If you "
"want to change this you can use the\n"
"`quarkus.http.root-path` config key to set the context path."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If you are using Servlet you can control the Servlet context path via "
"`quarkus.servlet.context-path`. This item is relative\n"
"to the http root above, and will only affect Servlet and things that run on "
"top of Servlet. Most applications will\n"
"want to use the HTTP root as this affects everything that Quarkus serves."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If both are specified then all non-Servlet web endpoints will be relative to "
"`quarkus.http.root-path`, while Servlet's\n"
"will be served relative to `{quarkus.http.root-path}/{quarkus.servlet."
"context-path}`."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If REST Assured is used for testing and `quarkus.http.root-path` is set then "
"Quarkus will automatically configure the\n"
"base URL for use in Quarkus tests, so test URL's should not include the root "
"path."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"In general, path configurations for web content are interpreted relative to "
"`quarkus.http.root-path` (which is / by default)."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To specify paths within this context root, use a relative path that does not "
"begin with a forward slash."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If you want to specify the URI explicitly, so it is always the same "
"regardless of the value of `quarkus.http.root-path`, use an absolute path "
"that begins with a forward slash."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"As an example, if an extension configures a `service` path, that endpoint "
"will be served from `${quarkus.http.root-path}/service`. If you change the "
"configuration of that path to `/service`, that endpoint will be served from "
"`/service`."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The link:https://quarkus.io/blog/path-resolution-in-quarkus/[Path Resolution "
"in Quarkus] blog post further explains how path resolution works for both "
"user and extension defined paths."
msgstr ""

msgid "Management Interface"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`quarkus.http.root-path` is only used for the main HTTP server.\n"
"If you enabled the management interface (using the `quarkus.management."
"enabled=true` property), you can configure the root path of the management "
"interface using:\n"
"`quarkus.management.root-path`."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Refer to the xref:./management-interface-reference.adoc[management interface "
"reference] for more information."
msgstr ""

msgid "Supporting secure connections with TLS/SSL"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To have Quarkus support secure connections, you must either provide a "
"certificate and associated key file, or supply a keystore."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"In both cases, a password must be provided. See the designated paragraph for "
"a detailed description of how to provide it."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To enable TLS/SSL support with native executables, please refer to our xref:"
"native-and-ssl.adoc[Using SSL With Native Executables guide]."
msgstr ""

msgid "Using the TLS centralized configuration"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Quarkus provides a xref:./tls-registry-reference.adoc[TLS centralized "
"configuration] that can be used to configure the server's TLS context.\n"
"It is the recommended approach to configure HTTPS."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To configure the HTTP server to use HTTPS, you can use the following "
"configuration:"
msgstr ""

msgid "To use a `p12` (PKCS12) key store, apply the following configuration:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Instead of the default configuration, you can use a named configuration:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Configuring the HTTP server directly"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If you do not want to use the TLS registry, you can configure the HTTP "
"server directly."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If the certificate has not been loaded into a keystore, it can be provided "
"directly using the properties listed below.\n"
"Quarkus will first try to load the given files as resources, and uses the "
"filesystem as a fallback.\n"
"The certificate / key pair will be loaded into a newly created keystore on "
"startup."
msgstr ""

msgid "Your `application.properties` would then look like this:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"An alternate solution is to directly provide a keystore which already "
"contains a default entry with a certificate.\n"
" You will need to at least provide the file and a password."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"As with the certificate/key file combination, Quarkus will first try to "
"resolve the given path as a resource, before attempting to read it from the "
"filesystem."
msgstr ""

msgid "Add the following property to your `application.properties`:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"As an optional hint, the type of keystore can be provided as one of the "
"options listed.\n"
"If the type is not provided, Quarkus will try to deduce it from the file "
"extensions."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"In both aforementioned scenarios, a password needs to be provided to create/"
"load the keystore with.\n"
"The password can be set in your `application.properties` (in plain-text) "
"using the following property:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"However, instead of providing the password as plain-text in the "
"configuration file (which is considered bad practice), it can instead be "
"supplied (using link:https://github.com/eclipse/microprofile-"
"config[MicroProfile Config])\n"
"as the environment variable "
"`QUARKUS_HTTP_SSL_CERTIFICATE_KEY_STORE_PASSWORD`.\n"
"This will also work in tandem with link:https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/"
"configuration/secret/#using-secrets-as-environment-variables[Kubernetes "
"secrets]."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"_Note: To remain compatible with earlier versions of Quarkus (before 0.16) "
"the default password is set to \"password\". It is therefore not a mandatory "
"parameter!_"
msgstr ""

msgid "Configure the HTTPS port"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"By default, Quarkus listens to port 8443 for SSL secured connections and "
"8444 when running tests."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"These ports can be configured in your `application.properties` with the "
"properties `quarkus.http.ssl-port` and `quarkus.http.test-ssl-port`."
msgstr ""

msgid "Disable the HTTP port"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"It is possible to disable the HTTP port and only support secure requests. "
"This is done via the\n"
"`quarkus.http.insecure-requests` property in `application.properties`. There "
"are three possible\n"
"values:"
msgstr ""

msgid "`enabled`"
msgstr ""

msgid "The default, HTTP works as normal"
msgstr ""

msgid "`redirect`"
msgstr ""

msgid "HTTP requests will be redirected to the HTTPS port"
msgstr ""

msgid "`disabled`"
msgstr ""

msgid "The HTTP port will not be opened."
msgstr ""

msgid "Reloading the certificates"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Key store, trust store and certificate files can be reloaded periodically.\n"
"Configure the `quarkus.http.ssl.certificate.reload-period` property to "
"specify the interval at which the certificates should be reloaded:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The files are reloaded from the same location as they were initially loaded "
"from.\n"
"If there is no content change, the reloading is a no-op.\n"
"It the reloading fails, the server will continue to use the previous "
"certificates."
msgstr ""

msgid "Preventing client renegotiation"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"See xref:./tls-registry-reference.adoc#client-renegotiation[Preventing "
"client renegotiation] for more information."
msgstr ""

msgid "Additional HTTP Headers"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To enable HTTP headers to be sent on every response, add the following "
"properties:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This will include the `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff` HTTP Header on "
"responses for requests performed on any resource in the application."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"You can also specify a `path` pattern and the HTTP `methods` the header "
"needs to be applied:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This will apply the `Pragma` header only when the `/headers/pragma` resource "
"is called with a `GET` or a `HEAD` method"
msgstr ""

msgid "Additional HTTP Headers per path"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If you need different header values depending on the path, you can use the "
"following configuration:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This will set the `Cache-Control` header to `none` when `/index.html` is "
"called."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"You can use a regular expression in the path and also specify the HTTP "
"methods where the HTTP header will be set:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"In case of overlapping paths in the configuration, you can specify an order "
"(higher values take precedence).\n"
"For example, having the following configuration:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Will include the `Cache-Control: max-age=1` header when `/paths/order` is "
"requested."
msgstr ""

msgid "Support 100-Continue in vert.x"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To support `100-continue`, the `quarkus.http.handle-100-continue-"
"automatically` option needs to be enabled explicitly\n"
"For additional information check https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/"
"rfc7231#section-5.1.1[100-continue] and the related\n"
"https://vertx.io/docs/apidocs/io/vertx/core/http/HttpServerOptions."
"html#DEFAULT_HANDLE_100_CONTINE_AUTOMATICALLY[Vert.x documentation]."
msgstr ""

msgid "HTTP/2 Support"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"HTTP/2 is enabled by default, and will be used by browsers if SSL is in use. "
"Even if SSL is not in use\n"
"HTTP/2 via cleartext upgrade is supported, and may be used by non-browser "
"clients."
msgstr ""

msgid "If you want to disable HTTP/2 you can set:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Note that some configuration attributes are specific to HTTP/2.\n"
"For example, to configure the max header list size (~ header), you need to "
"configure the `quarkus.http.limits.max-header-list-size` attribute.\n"
"You can also enable or disable HTTP/2 push using `quarkus.http.http2-push-"
"enabled`."
msgstr ""

msgid "Listening on a Random Port"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If you don't want to specify a port you can set `quarkus.http.port=0` or "
"`quarkus.http.test-port=0`. A random open port\n"
"will be picked by the OS, and a log message printed in the console."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To retrieve the real port, use the `io.quarkus.vertx.http.HttpServer` API, "
"which can be injected into the application\n"
"code as a CDI Bean:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Or into any kind of test, either as a field or test parameter:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To retrieve the `URI` that Quarkus is serving on, use "
"`HttpServer#getLocalBaseUri()`. In tests, the URI can be\n"
"directly injected with `@TestHTTPResource`:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Every Quarkus component that requires the actual http port, like REST "
"Assured, is automatically updated with the real\n"
"port."
msgstr ""

msgid "CORS filter"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To make your Quarkus application accessible to another application running "
"on a different domain, you need to configure cross-origin resource sharing "
"(CORS).\n"
"For more information about the CORS filter that Quarkus provides, see the "
"Quarkus xref:security-cors.adoc#cors-filter[CORS filter] section of the "
"\"Cross-origin resource sharing\" guide."
msgstr ""

msgid "HTTP Host header validation"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Quarkus can validate the HTTP `Host` header on incoming requests to protect "
"against DNS rebinding attacks and host header poisoning."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"When `quarkus.http.host` is set to a localhost name (`localhost`, `127.0.0."
"1`, or `[::1]`), host validation is automatically enabled in **dev** and "
"**production** modes.\n"
"Only requests whose `Host` header matches one of the known localhost names "
"are accepted; all others receive a `400 Bad Request` response."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This automatic behavior is a change from previous Quarkus versions, where no "
"`Host` header validation was performed.\n"
"If your application runs behind a reverse proxy that rewrites the `Host` "
"header, you may need to either:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"configure the allowed hosts explicitly with `quarkus.http.host-validation."
"allowed-hosts`, or"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"disable the automatic validation with `quarkus.http.host-validation.require-"
"localhost=false`."
msgstr ""

msgid "Configuring allowed hosts"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To restrict requests to a specific set of host names, use `allowed-hosts`:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The check is case-insensitive and only validates the host name, not the port."
""
msgstr ""

msgid "Requiring localhost only"
msgstr ""

msgid "To explicitly require that only localhost names are accepted:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To disable automatic localhost validation when the server is bound to a "
"localhost address:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Reverse proxy considerations"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"When running behind a reverse proxy, enable proxy header forwarding so that "
"Quarkus validates the forwarded host instead of the proxy's internal host "
"name:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This works with both the standard `Forwarded` header and the `X-Forwarded-"
"Host` header."
msgstr ""

msgid "HTTP Limits Configuration"
msgstr ""

msgid "Configure traffic shaping"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Traffic shaping allows you to limit the bandwidth across all channels (i.e. "
"connections), regardless of the number of open channels.\n"
"This can be useful when you want to control the overall network traffic to "
"prevent congestion or prioritize certain types of traffic."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To enable traffic shaping, add the following property in your application "
"configuration:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The traffic shaping allows you to configure various parameters, such as "
"write and read limitations (in bytes per  second), check interval (the delay "
"between two computations of the bandwidth), and maximum time to wait:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The check interval represents the period at which the traffic is computed, "
"and a higher interval may result in\n"
" less precise traffic shaping.\n"
"Despite 0 being accepted (no accounting), it is recommended to set a "
"positive value for the check interval, even if it is high since the "
"precision of the traffic shaping depends on the period where the traffic is "
"computed.\n"
"In this case, a suggested value is something close to 5 or 10 minutes."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The `outbound-global-bandwidth` and `inbound-global-bandwidth` parameters "
"represent the maximum number of bytes per second for write and read "
"operations, respectively.\n"
"You shall also consider to have object size in read or write operations "
"relatively adapted to the bandwidth you required.\n"
"For instance having 10 MB objects for 10KB/s will lead to burst effect, "
"while having 100 KB objects for 1 MB/s should be smoothly handle by the "
"traffic shaping."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Additionally, you can set the maximum time to wait (`max-delay`), which "
"specifies an upper bound for time shaping.\n"
"By default, it is set to 15 seconds.\n"
"It must be less than the HTTP timeout.\n"
"When one of the threshold is reached, no write happens for that period of "
"time."
msgstr ""

